    "media:imagetext": "CHINA NATIONAL AVIATION CORPORATION\nHead Office:\nChuen\nTien Bank Bldg.\nCHUNGKING\nOct. 29th, 1988\nPersonaL\nMr. S. I. Morgan,\nPan American Airways,\nNow York, N. I.\nDear liz. Morgan,\nEverything has been moving so rapidly the last week\nor so I have juat about lost all track of time, In my letter of Oct.\n19th I agein prediated the fall of Hanksow but I certainly bad no idea\nit would fell so soon.\nOn the twenty first we received sone urgent messages\nfrom the Minister who was in Hankow requesting us to immediately increase\nour service to lankow and advising us confidentially that the city wea\npersonal messages over the commercial telegraph from government officials\nI knew asicing me to arrange charter trips for them so it was evident the\nsituation in Hankow was getting tense.\nIt was impossible to increase our schedules with the\nflying boats as they were practically flying every day anyway so the\nonly thing we could do would be to use the DC-tros at night. We could go\ninto Hankow at night safely because the military field is equipped fot\nnight flying and the country all around is flat, but night flying into\nChungking is impossible except on very rare occasions.\nWe therefore would\nhave to fly from Hankow to Chengtu.\nWe therefore agreed to fly from\nChungking to Hankow, Leaving so as to arrive in Henkow soon after dark and\nthen fly to Chengtu and back to lankow arriving just before day light and\nthen make a second flight out of Honkow for Chungking•\nHe decided to drop\nthe steward and take fifteen passengers. This was a rather anbitious\nschedule and would require every break in order to accomplish it. We only\nhad two pilots with night flying experience who could do it, Sharp and\nLeonard and Sherp was in Hongkong. Aiso there were no night landing\nfacilities at Chengtu, so\nwe would have to arrange for them. We therefore\nsent Leonard to Hongkong\nthat afternoon, he was scheduled to go anyway, with\ninstructions to return that night with Sharp. I sent Sharp a messege to\nget a floodlight that would work from a storage battery, and be prepared to\nreturn to Chungking with Leonard. They both arrived about nine o'clock on\nthe morning of the 22nd. I discussed the situation with them and they\nthought it could be done. Leonard who had been flying nearly sil night\nwent up to where I live and went to bed. He sent Nich to Chengtu with the\nflood 1ight and a lot of lanterns to arrange for the 1ighte and to have gos\nand o11 and a service crew on the field. Just as Sharp was leaving the",
